WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:04.620
So to wrap up this portion of the hands-on lab,

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.419
there's a couple more things that we want to cover.

3
00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:11.539
Now the first is, if you recall, when we created our SKM bot

4
00:00:11.539 --> 00:00:14.619
and added it to our sequence, we

5
00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:17.460
had converted it to a replaceable.

6
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.859
The question is, well, how do we actually replace that

7
00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:21.100
at runtime?

8
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.260
So what I'm going to do is actually just unlock

9
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.140
my viewport here.

10
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.269
So I've got a free camera.

11
00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:28.989
And I'm going to just find some place kind

12
00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:30.309
of hidden in the world.

13
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.109
We'll just go over to this location here.

14
00:00:33.109 --> 00:00:35.270
And I'm going to go to my content browser.

15
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.789
And I'm going to go down to our bots.

16
00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:39.990
And it should be our mesh.

17
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.030
And I'm looking for this SKM bot runtime.

18
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.829
We'll go ahead and just drag him into the level.

19
00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:48.429
Now, real quick note here, SKM bot runtime

20
00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:52.070
is really no different from our SKM bot in the sense

21
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:56.189
that the only thing that's changed is the coloration.

22
00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.710
With spawnables, you will more than likely

23
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.189
want to target, say, for example, a character blueprint that

24
00:01:03.189 --> 00:01:05.870
will be whatever the player is using at runtime,

25
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.870
as opposed to what we're doing here with just adding

26
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.709
an actor to the level.

27
00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:14.349
Now, one other caveat here is in subsequent versions

28
00:01:14.349 --> 00:01:16.670
of the engine, this may change

29
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.069
to where you don't have to add the actor to the level.

30
00:01:19.069 --> 00:01:21.230
You may be able to target from the content browser

31
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.510
or load something.

32
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:24.209
But for the purposes of this class,

33
00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:25.750
we're just going to drag it in here

34
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.189
so we can easily target the actor.

35
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.780
All right, let's go back to our little platform

36
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.980
here where we have our animation,

37
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:34.980
back to our sequence.

38
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.180
So here's our SKM bot.

39
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.700
Now, to do the replacement, we actually

40
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.500
need to drag the sequence into our level.

41
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.859
So go back to our content browser drawer

42
00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:47.340
here in our sequence example folder.

43
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.140
And in this case, the one I'm working with

44
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.620
is my sequence 105 example.

45
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.260
I'm just going to drag this in here

46
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.480
because this is the location, the position,

47
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.840
and this is where we'll do it.

48
00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:02.000
So in the Sequencer Detail panel, so again,

49
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.599
if you don't have that, just click the actor.

50
00:02:04.599 --> 00:02:07.760
We're going to scroll down, and what we're looking for

51
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.800
is our binding overrides.

52
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.960
So I'm going to click a little plus button here to add it.

53
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.030
We'll expand this out.

54
00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.710
Object binding LD, you'll see, is an unresolved binding.

55
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.870
So if we click that, you'll notice

56
00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.990
that we have the two actors from within our sequence.

57
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.110
So there's our cinema camera actor and our SKM bot.

58
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.189
This is going to be the actor that we

59
00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:30.949
are replacing at runtime.

60
00:02:30.949 --> 00:02:33.610
Of course, we want our SKM bot.

61
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.650
And then the bound actor is going

62
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.090
to be that new skeletal mesh that we added.

63
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.530
So we could click this down from here and try to find it.

64
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.009
Or if we fly back, what I can do

65
00:02:45.009 --> 00:02:47.169
is just kind of keep this in viewport,

66
00:02:47.169 --> 00:02:50.689
click the little eyedropper, click our little bot runtime,

67
00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:52.689
and then that should be good to go.

68
00:02:52.689 --> 00:02:53.689
All right.

69
00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:57.810
So now, if we go over to our sequence,

70
00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.479
now again, if I preview this and hit play,

71
00:03:01.479 --> 00:03:04.599
you notice that the actor is still the same.

72
00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:06.919
And that's because we're viewing the preview.

73
00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:10.520
If I go back, and let's go ahead and just simulate this,

74
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.520
which should put it in runtime.

75
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:13.680
There we go.

76
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.039
You can see the game is moving.

77
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:16.879
I'm just going to press G for game mode.

78
00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:19.000
And you can already see it off screen here.

79
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.080
But now if I hit play, because again, we

80
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.479
are running in real time, that now the character has

81
00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:26.960
been switched with that mesh.

82
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.840
All right, hopefully that makes sense.

83
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.560
Now, one thing to note here, let's

84
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.530
go ahead and stop the simulation.

85
00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.509
One thing to note here is that if you

86
00:03:34.509 --> 00:03:38.650
try to have multiple actors, so multiple of these SKM bots,

87
00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:43.129
and you are swapping them with the same swapped mesh,

88
00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:47.449
just note that right now in, I believe we're in 5.5.3,

89
00:03:47.449 --> 00:03:49.650
there's a limitation that it won't actually swap it.

90
00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:52.050
So you can only have one actor that

91
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.409
is referenced to another actor.

92
00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:56.169
There are many pros and cons to that.

93
00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:59.810
But for now, just know that for our final shot,

94
00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:05.080
so if I go back to our 905 sequence final,

95
00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.000
you notice in the sequence itself that we only

96
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.520
have the one character.

97
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.520
But of course, on the actual sequence itself,

98
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.479
we're swapping it with an additional mesh.

99
00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:16.160
So hopefully that makes sense.

100
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.240
OK, let's go ahead and go back to our 105 sequence.

101
00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.279
And we're going to run over just a couple other important

102
00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:26.529
things to keep in mind here for organization.

103
00:04:26.529 --> 00:04:28.769
All right, so within my sequence itself,

104
00:04:28.769 --> 00:04:31.209
now for this hands-on lab, we're

105
00:04:31.209 --> 00:04:33.610
actually building all three of our shots

106
00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.569
in a single sequence.

107
00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:36.730
You can choose to do that.

108
00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.089
You can choose to set them up as multiples.

109
00:04:39.089 --> 00:04:42.009
We're keeping it simple for the purposes of this class.

110
00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:44.110
So we'll have them together.

111
00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.870
So another way that we can say organize,

112
00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.910
I'm going to select my SKM bot, right click.

113
00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:51.629
And then what I want to do is create.

114
00:04:51.629 --> 00:04:55.430
Now, I could do add a group or a different tag

115
00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.949
if you want to stay organized.

116
00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:57.949
Great way to do that.

117
00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:01.110
Or I can just right click in the empty space, add folder,

118
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.310
and I'll just call this characters,

119
00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.589
something like that.

120
00:05:05.589 --> 00:05:07.589
Drag our SKM bot up to here.

121
00:05:07.589 --> 00:05:10.310
And then maybe I want to create another folder.

122
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:14.149
So right clicking and call this cinema or something

123
00:05:14.149 --> 00:05:17.509
for cameras and drag my camera in there.

124
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.069
And you see that, of course, we can move these

125
00:05:19.069 --> 00:05:21.629
and organize them, nest them together.

126
00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:25.470
Another thing just for easily recognizability

127
00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.910
is I can right click on the folder and do set color.

128
00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:31.230
So maybe I want to change my character to something

129
00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.430
like pink.

130
00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:36.949
Maybe my camera, I want to do something like yellow.

131
00:05:36.949 --> 00:05:40.269
Of course, now I can roll these up and you see I can stay a little more

132
00:05:40.269 --> 00:05:42.629
organized.

133
00:05:42.629 --> 00:05:44.550
One other thing to keep in mind here, again,

134
00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.149
for the purposes of this class, we

135
00:05:46.189 --> 00:05:50.230
are creating all three of our shots in a single sequence.

136
00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.350
But you may be working in an environment

137
00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.310
where you have multiple different departments working

138
00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.149
on a shot at the same time.

139
00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:00.470
So for example, you may have an effects department,

140
00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.029
you may have an animation department,

141
00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:04.459
you may have an environment department.

142
00:06:04.459 --> 00:06:08.379
One thing that really helps to keep your files organized,

143
00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:10.740
as well as the ability to concurrently work

144
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.339
on a single sequence, is using,

145
00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:16.420
so I'll right click on here, a sub sequence track.

146
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.980
All this is is you're just adding another sequence

147
00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.620
to your sequence nested under here, which again could

148
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.779
be something just easily named as like characters,

149
00:06:25.779 --> 00:06:27.339
environment, et cetera.

150
00:06:27.339 --> 00:06:29.819
We cover that in the intro, but just keep that in mind

151
00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:31.420
when it comes to organization.

152
00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:33.180
That is probably something that you definitely

153
00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.660
want to use in production.
